-- Migration: grimoire_species
-- 23 playable library species written from scratch for Dungeon Grimoire (#955, 9-10 Oct 2026):
-- Goblin, Kobold, Aasimar, Changeling, Shifter, Loxodon, Firbolg, Elementborn, Hexborn, Shellfolk, Hobgoblin, Bugbear, Birdfolk, Corvid Folk, Lizardfolk, Triton, Centaur, Satyr, Fairy, Starfarer, Stillwarden, Serpentkin, Owlfolk.
--
-- Why our own text: none of these has an openly licensed playable version. The SRD (2014 and 2024) has goblins,
-- kobolds, lizardfolk and the like only as monsters; the familiar player versions are in Wizards of the Coast's
-- non-SRD books (and Black Flag's open reference document does not include them). Game mechanics are not protected;
-- their wording, distinctive trait names, names of WotC peoples and setting lore are. So these rows follow familiar
-- effect shapes in original wording, with original trait names and original lore. Peoples whose names are WotC's own
-- are renamed (Elementborn, Hexborn, Shellfolk, Birdfolk, Corvid Folk, Owlfolk, Starfarer, Stillwarden, Serpentkin).
-- Every species picture was painted for this set (sunburst, race showcase series).
--
-- They live under their own source, `grimoire-species`, titled "Grimoire Species (homebrew)": a set of its own, apart
-- from every book and from our other bundled content, so a DM opts in to our species alone and can see at a glance that
-- they are informal house content, not a published book. Like any non-SRD source it is opt-in per campaign
-- (campaign_enabled_sources); `source` is what fetchLibrarySpecies filters on. One row per ruleset, sharing
-- conceptual_key: the 2024 rows carry no species ability scores (the 2024 rules put them on backgrounds), the 2014
-- rows carry fixed increases as 2014 species do.

values ('grimoire-species', null, 'Grimoire Species (homebrew)', 'Dungeon Grimoire', '{}', 'Original homebrew species written for Dungeon Grimoire. Not from any published book.', null, null, true, true, 83)
on conflict (key) do nothing;

on conflict (id) do nothing;
